The emerald cut vs asscher cut diamond comparison trips up more buyers than almost any other shape decision. Both are step cuts. Both have cropped corners.

Both require VS1 clarity and G+ color. On paper, they sound like near-identical choices.

They are not.

The single biggest difference between them is not sparkle, not vintage appeal, and not price. It is face-up size. For the same carat weight, an emerald cut shows 25–30% more surface area on the finger than an Asscher. That gap is visible to anyone standing across a room.

This guide covers the optical difference, the face-up size math, clarity and color floors, natural and lab price data, settings, and a plain verdict on which cut fits which buyer.

---

**TL;DR**

- Both are step cuts — the same facet architecture, the same clarity floor (VS1+), the same color floor (G+)
- Emerald cut shows 25–30% more face-up area at the same carat weight — the single biggest practical difference
- Asscher has a unique optical pattern (Hall of Mirrors Effect) that the emerald cut does not replicate
- Natural 1ct H-VS1: [emerald from ~$4,200](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) / [asscher from ~$4,900](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=asscher&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)
- Lab 1ct D-VVS1: [emerald from ~$900](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&LabGrown=true&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) / [asscher from ~$1,050](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=asscher&LabGrown=true&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)
- → For maximum face-up size: choose emerald cut. → For the hall-of-mirrors optical pattern: choose Asscher.
- Priority: decide what you want on the finger — visible size or visual character. Everything else follows from that.

---

## Expert Summary

Both the emerald cut and Asscher cut are members of the step-cut family. They share a facet architecture built on long parallel tiers rather than the triangular facets used in brilliant cuts. Neither is good at hiding inclusions or body color — VS1 and G are the practical minimums for both.

The divergence is shape. The emerald cut is rectangular, typically 1.30–1.50 length-to-width. The Asscher cut is square, typically 1.00–1.05 length-to-width.

That shape difference produces a face-up size gap that persists at every carat weight.

A 1ct emerald cut measures approximately 6.5×4.5mm. A 1ct Asscher measures approximately 5.5×5.5mm. The emerald's table covers roughly 29mm².

The Asscher's table covers roughly 30mm². They are nearly identical in raw area — but the emerald's elongated silhouette reads as larger on the hand because human perception overestimates rectangular shapes relative to square ones at the same stated dimension.

At 2ct, the gap widens further. The emerald cut's longer table becomes unmistakably larger on the finger. The Asscher, by contrast, sits more compactly — which some buyers prefer for everyday wear.

The Asscher's other defining trait is optical. Its deeply trimmed corners and stacked square facets create a concentric reflection pattern — the Hall of Mirrors Effect — that no other cut replicates. If that visual is what you want, the Asscher is the only stone that delivers it.

If you want the most finger presence for your budget, choose emerald. If you want the Asscher's specific optical character, no substitute exists.

---

## What Is an Emerald Cut Diamond?

The emerald cut was originally developed for cutting emerald gemstones — a soft stone that chips under the pressure required for brilliant faceting. Applied to diamonds during the Art Deco era of the 1920s, the cut became a staple of minimal, architectural jewelry.

The structure: a rectangular table with step facets running parallel to the girdle in tiers, trimmed corners at all four edges, and a shallow crown. The facets do not scatter light. They reflect it in long, slow planes — broad flashes alternating with dark contrast areas as the stone moves.

The result is what buyers describe as "elegant," "architectural," and "cinematic." It is the opposite of the rapid-fire sparkle of a round brilliant.

![Emerald cut diamond facet structure diagram — step facets, parallel tiers, open table](/images/emerald-cut-diamond-facet-structure-diagram.avif)

The typical L:W ratio runs 1.30–1.50:1. A 1ct emerald cut measures approximately 6.5×4.5mm. A 2ct emerald measures approximately 8.5×6.0mm.

The elongated shape runs lengthwise across the finger, which creates the optical illusion of length on the hand.

---

## What Is an Asscher Cut Diamond?

The Asscher cut was created by Joseph Asscher in Amsterdam in 1902. It remained a niche cut until the early 2000s, when a wave of Art Deco jewelry nostalgia brought it back into mainstream demand.

The shape: square, with deeply trimmed corners that produce an octagonal outline. The crown is higher than an emerald cut's, which adds brilliance relative to the flatter step cuts. The pavilion facets are arranged in concentric squares, stacked from the girdle down to the culet.

The signature visual is the Hall of Mirrors Effect — covered in detail in its own section below.

![Asscher cut diamond shape vs emerald cut — L:W ratio comparison, corner trim depth](/images/asscher-vs-emerald-cut-shape-comparison.avif)

A 1ct Asscher cut measures approximately 5.5×5.5mm. The square outline keeps all the carat weight compact — nothing extends out along the finger. A 2ct Asscher measures approximately 7.0×7.0mm.

Asscher cuts are rarer than emerald cuts. Fewer cutters specialize in them, and the demand has historically been lower. That rarity does not automatically translate to higher prices — but it does mean fewer options in any given quality tier.

---

## The Square Trap

Here is the thing most buyers comparing these two cuts miss entirely.

They know one is rectangular and one is square. They assume that is a preference — do you want a longer shape or a compact shape? What they do not know is the face-up size consequence of that choice.

A square stone concentrates its mass differently than a rectangular stone. An Asscher cut at 1ct puts roughly 5.5mm on each side. An emerald cut at 1ct runs 6.5mm long.

On the finger, that 6.5mm length is visually dominant — the eye reads it as a large stone. The Asscher's 5.5mm sits quietly, reading closer to a 0.70ct round brilliant in terms of perceived size.

This is the Square Trap: buyers choose the Asscher because they like the look in a photo, not realizing they are trading away 25–30% of visible finger coverage per dollar spent.

