The gap is real and consistent across carat weights. At 0.50ct, both cuts are small — the emerald's elongation is modest, but visible. At 2ct, the difference is stark.

An emerald cut buyer at 2ct gets a stone that fills the finger. An Asscher buyer at 2ct gets a stone that sits squarely, attractively, but noticeably more compact.

Neither outcome is wrong. But you should choose it intentionally, not accidentally.

If you want the Asscher look and the compact square silhouette, buy the Asscher knowing you are paying for visual character, not visual size. If you want maximum finger presence for your budget, the emerald cut wins at every carat weight.

