The premium compounds at higher carat weights. At 3ct, the Asscher buyer pays roughly $6,000 more for a stone that shows less finger coverage.

That is not a bad trade if the Hall of Mirrors Effect is what you are buying. It is a bad trade if you did not know the size difference existed.

Color note: Asscher cuts are sometimes recommended at G rather than H because the higher crown concentrates color in the center of the square table. Budget for G color if the stone will be set in white gold or platinum.

> **Farzana says:** "I tell clients to hold both cuts next to each other on their hand before they decide anything. The size difference hits them immediately. The Asscher is always the one that surprises them — they expected it to look the same. Once they see the difference, the decision is much easier."

