## Budget Breakdown: Which Cut at Every Price Point

The price difference between these two cuts scales with budget. Here is how to think about it at each tier.

**Under $2,000 (lab, ~0.50–0.75ct):** Both cuts are affordable here with lab stones. A [0.50ct D-VVS1 lab emerald cut](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&LabGrown=true&caratWeight=0.5&color=D&clarity=VVS1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) runs ~[$350](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&LabGrown=true&caratWeight=0.5&color=D&clarity=VVS1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). A [0.50ct D-VVS1 lab Asscher](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=asscher&LabGrown=true&caratWeight=0.5&color=D&clarity=VVS1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) runs ~[$400](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=asscher&LabGrown=true&caratWeight=0.5&color=D&clarity=VVS1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). The difference is small enough that the choice is purely shape preference. At this size, both cuts are modest — choose the shape you love.

**$2,000–$5,000 (natural 1ct or lab 1.5–2ct):** This is where the decision sharpens. For natural stones, [1ct H-VS1 emerald](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&caratWeight=1.0&color=H&clarity=VS1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) at ~[$4,200](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&caratWeight=1.0&color=H&clarity=VS1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) vs [1ct H-VS1 Asscher](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=asscher&caratWeight=1.0&color=H&clarity=VS1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) at ~[$4,900](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=asscher&caratWeight=1.0&color=H&clarity=VS1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). The $700 premium buys the Asscher's unique optical character and nothing else. If the Hall of Mirrors Effect is the priority, pay it. If size on the finger is the priority, do not.

For lab at this budget, a [1.5ct D-VVS1 lab emerald](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&LabGrown=true&caratWeight=1.5&color=D&clarity=VVS1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) runs ~[$1,500](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&LabGrown=true&caratWeight=1.5&color=D&clarity=VVS1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). That is a 7.5×5.5mm stone — significantly more finger coverage than any 1ct cut.

**$5,000–$15,000 (natural 1.5–2ct or lab 2–3ct):** At this level, the Asscher premium is meaningful. A [2ct H-VS1 natural Asscher](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=asscher&caratWeight=2.0&color=H&clarity=VS1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) at ~[$17,000](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=asscher&caratWeight=2.0&color=H&clarity=VS1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) costs $2,500 more than the [2ct H-VS1 natural emerald](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&caratWeight=2.0&color=H&clarity=VS1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) at ~[$14,500](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&caratWeight=2.0&color=H&clarity=VS1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). The Asscher also shows less finger coverage. Most buyers at this tier who are undecided should choose the emerald cut unless they specifically want the Asscher optical effect.

**Above $15,000 (natural 3ct+):** Budget sensitivity decreases. At 3ct, both cuts are statement stones. The [3ct H-VS1 natural emerald](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&caratWeight=3.0&color=H&clarity=VS1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) at ~[$35,000](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&caratWeight=3.0&color=H&clarity=VS1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) fills the hand completely. The [3ct H-VS1 natural Asscher](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=asscher&caratWeight=3.0&color=H&clarity=VS1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) at ~[$41,000](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=asscher&caratWeight=3.0&color=H&clarity=VS1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is a compact, intensely geometric stone with the Hall of Mirrors Effect at full expression. Both are exceptional choices at this level — this is a visual preference decision, not a value decision.

