## Who Should Choose Which: 4 Buyer Scenarios

**Best for maximum finger presence:** Choose the emerald cut. At every carat weight, the elongated table runs visibly along the finger. A [1.5ct H-VS1 emerald cut](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&caratWeight=1.5&color=H&clarity=VS1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) at ~[$8,500](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&caratWeight=1.5&color=H&clarity=VS1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) shows more finger coverage than a 2ct Asscher at ~[$17,000](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=asscher&caratWeight=2.0&color=H&clarity=VS1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). For buyers who want a visibly large stone, the choice is clear.

**Best for the hall-of-mirrors optical look:** Choose the Asscher. Nothing else produces this effect. If you saw the concentric-square reflection pattern on someone's hand and it is what drew you to this comparison, buy the Asscher. The emerald cut will not satisfy you as a substitute. A [1ct G-VS1 lab asscher](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=asscher&LabGrown=true&caratWeight=1.0&color=G&clarity=VS1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) at ~[$1,050](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=asscher&LabGrown=true&caratWeight=1.0&color=G&clarity=VS1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the most cost-effective entry point.

**Best for vintage or Art Deco aesthetic:** Choose the Asscher. The cut was designed for that era. Art Deco settings — geometric milgrain, engraved platinum, filigree — align with the Asscher's octagonal outline and concentric geometry. An emerald cut in a vintage setting looks good. An Asscher in a vintage setting looks like it was made for the ring. See [asscher cut vintage settings](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings?MetalType=all&Diamonds=Dia&ShapeID=Asscher&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

**Best for maximum clarity and color per dollar (lab):** Choose the emerald cut. A [2ct D-VVS1 lab emerald cut](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&LabGrown=true&caratWeight=2.0&color=D&clarity=VVS1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) at ~[$2,400](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&LabGrown=true&caratWeight=2.0&color=D&clarity=VVS1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) delivers maximum step-cut face-up area, colorless appearance, and clean step facets. The Asscher lab equivalent costs ~[$400](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=asscher&LabGrown=true&caratWeight=2.0&color=D&clarity=VVS1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) more for less face-up area. Unless the Asscher visual is specifically what you want, the emerald cut delivers better optical value per dollar.

> **Farzana says:** "Almost every buyer who comes in asking about the Asscher already knows which one they want — they just need permission to buy it. The hall-of-mirrors look is very specific. If that is what you are chasing, the emerald cut will not satisfy you. But if you are undecided and you care about size on the hand, I always show them both cuts next to each other on the same finger. The emerald wins that comparison every time."

